{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>The hidden things belong to HaShem our Elohim</b>. According to the opinion of the commentators, it says that HaShem our Elohim — it is His to execute judgment upon those who practice idolatry in secret, for all hidden things are revealed before Him, and <b>the revealed things belong to us and to our children</b> to perform for them all the words of this Torah — to strike those who practice idolatry according to the law of the Torah. And according to the Midrash (Sanhedrin 43) as well, the same is so. But my opinion according to the plain sense is that <b>the hidden things</b> are the sins hidden from those who commit them, as in <i>Who can discern errors? From hidden ones acquit me</i> (Psalms 19:13) — it says: the hidden things belong to HaShem alone; we bear no guilt for sin committed through them. But the revealed things, which are the willful transgressions, belong to us and to our children forever, to perform all the words of this Torah as an everlasting statute, for thus we accepted upon ourselves, both <b>those who are here with us</b> and <b>those who are not here with us</b>, for all generations. And since he introduced into the curse the obligation to perform all the commandment, he thereby excluded from the ban one who acts in inadvertence, so that he is not included in this curse. And the words of Onkelos (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 29:28) incline in this direction, for he said <i>דְּמִטַּמְרָן קֳדָם ה' אֱלָהַנָא</i> (\"that are hidden before HaShem our Elohim\"), and were it according to the words of the commentators, it would have been proper for him to say <i>דְּמִטַּמְּרִין לַה' אֱלָהַנָא</i> (\"that are hidden to HaShem our Elohim\")."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:56:53.456958611Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}